{"name":"Old Prune","hex":"#8272a4","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"old","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Mochi."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of an object, concept, relationship, etc., having existed for a relatively long period of time.","Of a living being, having lived for most of the expected years."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/old"},{"term":"prune","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plum.","The dried, wrinkled fruit of certain species of plum."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To become wrinkled like a dried plum, as the fingers and toes do when kept submerged in water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/prune"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prune","blocks":[{"text":"A prune is a dried plum, most commonly from the European plum tree. Not all plum species or varieties can be dried into prunes. Use of the term prune for fresh plums is obsolete except when applied to varieties of plum grown for drying. In this usage, a prune is the firm-fleshed plum fruit of P. domestica varieties that have a high soluble solids content and do not ferment during drying."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Rosales_-_Dried_Prunus_domestica_d.jpg/330px-Rosales_-_Dried_Prunus_domestica_d.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prune","wikidata":"Q6766526"},{"title":"Plum (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Plum is a purple color with a brownish-gray tinge, like that shown on the right, or a reddish purple, which is a close representation of the average color of the plum fruit."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7b/Plum_on_tree02.jpg/330px-Plum_on_tree02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plum_(color)","wikidata":"Q3108722"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.